![South elevation, night, after the pass](reports/exterior/tour_after/south_front_s.png)

**The defect that dominated every exterior view**

A hard-edged dark disc hung over the roof in every frame. The sky dome is a 200 m sphere centred on the world origin and the camera's far plane is 240 m, so once the player is more than about 40 m from the house the far side of the dome is clipped and the hole projects as a circle of clear colour in the starfield. The stars sit on a 190 m sphere inside the dome, which is why it read as an unlit dome rather than as a hole. The sky group now follows the camera each frame in [main.js](src/main.js), so the dome is never sliced; direction, stars and moon are unchanged. The disc is gone in all six tour frames.

**What I added to the elevation** ([facade.js](src/world/facade.js))

Cast-iron rainwater goods at the mansard's lower edge on all four elevations: a half-round trough with an outer lip and hanger brackets, a hopper head and leader under the eave, a bracketed stack down the wall face, and a shoe above the plinth. Stack stations are tested against the real window list and skipped wherever they would cross an opening. The trough sits outside the 0.88 m projecting slate edge, which is where that roof actually throws its water.

Capiz-screened windows on the principal south and east openings of the ground and first floors: a translucent shell panel set inside the reveal, with a dark-timber lattice at 0.30 m uprights and two rails. Shell is its own material — rough, slightly emissive, double-sided — because shell scatters and glass does not. The rear and service side keep plain glazing, which is how the expensive material would have been spent on a house of this period.

