Fix design:
For each storey above B1, when a room's exterior-facing boundary lies inside the perimeter (i.e., between room and perimeter wall there's a void), the perimeter windows should be transferred to that inner wall: i.e., the window opening must be cut in BOTH walls and a deep reveal built. But window spacing is generated along the perimeter independent of rooms, so an inner wall may not have a window aligned with the void window. Simplest robust approach: cut the same opening in any interior wall segment that lies within the void zone behind a perimeter window, provided geometry permits; add jamb/head/sill reveals spanning the gap between the inner and outer wall faces so the deep opening reads as a thick wall.

