The exterior is now the manor from the reference rather than a castle, and every window opening measures clean in the live scene.

## What was actually wrong, measured in the running game

| Symptom | Cause found |
|---|---|
| "Giant flat green wall" | The ivy mesh bounded x −26.5…27.7, y −0.3…11.9: six clusters of 13 overlapping sheets each, carpeting most of the south elevation |
| Repetitive vertical strips | `pilasters()` built an 11.55 m stone shaft between *every* pair of windows, 11 per façade, all in the pale dressing stone |
| Pale slabs above the roofline | `rangeRoof()` rise = half-span × 0.95; a 22 m span gave a 10.4 m rise, so pavilion roofs stood 5.6 m above the mansard as masonry pyramids (stone mesh reached y = 25.2 while slate topped at 19.6) |
| "Almost entirely black" | Midnight sky (zenith 0x1d2a35, 1 300 stars), background 0x0a0f14, nothing lighting the south face — the wall sampled at RGB (30, 45, 38) against the reference's mid-grey |
| Wall over an F4 window | The front gable's triangle was 7 cm too narrow at the west window's head, so the all-or-nothing aperture punch skipped it and 14 cm of masonry sat in front of the glass |
| Blank west elevation | `MAX_REVEAL = 1.15` refused every window in the F2/F3 rows, which stand 2 m back from the perimeter |

## Fixes

- **Shouldered gable** instead of a pure triangle: vertical cheeks to y = 14.5, rake to 16.6 m, 8.3 m wide. Both flanking attic windows are now wholly inside the face, get real holes and their own stone casings, jambs, hoods and sills.
- **Pavilion roofs capped** at a 4.2 m rise, so they sit an attic storey above the eave.
- **Ivy cut back** to two shaded end clusters that stop ~6.9 m up, with two short runners — damp staining in the material now carries the weathering.
- **Pilasters reduced** to one corner pier per face, 0.46 × 0.16 m, so the wall is masonry again rather than a stripe pattern.
- **`MAX_REVEAL` 1.15 → 2.15**, giving the inset storeys real windows in lined 2 m reveals (67 → 71 windows).
- **Overcast dusk sky** sampled from the reference (horizon 0xa6b8c2, zenith 0x5d7385), stars and the moon disc removed, moon kept as diffused cloud glow; background and fog retuned to match, with a south-west fill light so the entrance elevation reads.

## Verification, in the browser

`scripts/wallwin_runtime_audit.cjs`: **71 windows, 639 rays, 639 hit glass first, 0 wall, 0 trim, 0 no-hit.** `npm run build` exit 0. The entrance doors resolved through the game's own target picker and swung 0 → 1.35 rad over 20 distinct steps, then back to 0.009 — hinges and state machine intact. Interior frames confirm the raised key did not blow out rooms.

![Front elevation](reports/exterior_gothic/front.png)
![Three-quarter](reports/exterior_gothic/threequarter.png)
![Entrance](reports/exterior_gothic/porch.png)
![West elevation](reports/exterior_gothic/west.png)

## Honest limitations

The 1.30 M triangle count is unchanged by this pass and is high for an Intel HD 4600 — measured 34–58 fps in headless *software* rendering, which does not predict the real GPU. The side elevations are still simpler than the front: the mansard reads as one hipped mass from east and west, where the reference has cross-gables at the range ends. Keyboard walking could not be exercised headless (pointer lock needs a user gesture), so movement was checked through the game's handlers rather than WASD. Full notes are in [REPORT.md](reports/exterior_gothic/REPORT.md).

The next most valuable step is breaking up the east and west roof mass, since that is the view the player gets while walking the approach.
