The mansion's envelope is now a Filipino ancestral house instead of a Victorian manor, and it still boots and walks. Here is what I found and what I changed.

## What the project actually contained

The mansion was already built, furnished and validated: 57 rooms across a basement, ground, three upper floors and a rooftop level; 66 doors; a spiral stair tower and a basement flight; an inner courtyard; a full furniture set from a 26-piece Blender GLB; and a QA suite that walks every floor. So a from-scratch rebuild would have destroyed working content the brief itself tells me to preserve.

What it did **not** have was the right architecture. [facade.js](src/world/facade.js) was written to a source comment beginning "a decayed high-Victorian manor — steep slate pitches, gables at every range end, a round tower with a conical cap at the corner, a chimney row". That is a European château, which Section 3 forbids outright. So I rebuilt the envelope and left the plan, interiors, stairs, colliders, roof deck, ladder route and underground connector untouched.

## The house now

- **Stone ground floor** — rubble base course, stone corner piers, coursed stone walls.
- **Two timber storeys** — board siding in stained narra, with a moulded band and drip at each floor line.
- **Limewashed attic** above, and the rooftop edge is now an open azotea behind the stone-and-iron parapet: the fourth-floor perimeter wall is only built where a pavilion actually stands against it or a door is cut (`deckSpans()`), so the roofs show above the balustrade instead of hiding inside a three-metre box.
- **Capiz windows**: dark architrave, two sliding shell sashes, fixed shell transom, louvred persiana folded against the wall, ventanilla of slatted panels under a projecting board sill.
- **Hipped pavilion roofs** in weathered corrugated iron with 0.95 m eaves, exposed rafter tails, fascia and ridge caps; **timber portico** on turned posts with a calado frieze and a 30° hipped canopy; **boarded balconies** on joists and brackets instead of concrete shelves; fern clumps and downpipes at the foot of the walls.

![The house from the drive](reports/rebuild_final/s/south_900.png)
![Entrance, portico and capiz windows](reports/rebuild_close/s/02_entrance.png)
![North elevation: stone, boards, attic, hips](reports/rebuild_final/s/north_face.png)

## Files changed

[facade.js](src/world/facade.js) (rewritten), [materials.js](src/world/materials.js) (board and corrugated-iron materials, limewash, corrected tile sizes), [architecture.js](src/world/architecture.js) (per-storey exterior material, azotea parapet split, timber balconies, exterior UV scale fix — every exterior stone was 2.5× life size). Details in [REPORT.md](reports/mansion_rebuild/REPORT.md).

## Verified, not assumed

`npx vite build` passes (997 kB / 283 kB gzip). The headless boot reports no page errors — the fallback warning did catch two real bugs during the work (`B is not defined` in `hipRoof`, a missing argument in `pavilionRoofs`), both fixed. `scripts/audit_mansion.cjs` reports 66 doors, no missing doors, no door without a collider, 1169 colliders, no errors. `scripts/roof_route.cjs` still climbs the ladder to the roof deck and back down (349 frames up, door, deck, return, 349 down). `scripts/scan_apps.cjs` passes. Screenshots were captured from the running renderer.

## What I did not do

The interiors are the existing, already-QA'd set — no bespoke per-room prop pass this turn. There is **no `.blend` for the house**: the mansion is generated by code (`plan.js` → `architecture.js` → `facade.js`), and the `Blender/AssetLibrary/Working/House` build folder referenced by `npm run build:house` is absent from this tree and not inside `Blender.zip` — producing one would mean re-modelling, not exporting, so I did not fabricate it. No new assets were downloaded; everything uses the Poly Haven textures the project already licenses.
